    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.113 UBTU-24-700010"
      info        : "The operating system must generate error messages that provide information necessary for corrective actions without revealing information that could be exploited by adversaries.

GROUP ID: V-270756
RULE ID: SV-270756r1066757

Any operating system providing too much information in error messages risks compromising the data and security of the structure, and content of error messages needs to be carefully considered by the organization.

Organizations carefully consider the structure/content of error messages. The extent to which information systems are able to identify and handle error conditions is guided by organizational policy and operational requirements. Information that could be exploited by adversaries includes, for example, erroneous logon attempts with passwords entered by mistake as the username, mission/business information that can be derived from (if not stated explicitly by) information recorded, and personal information, such as account numbers, social security numbers, and credit card numbers.

The /var/log/btmp, /var/log/wtmp, and /var/log/lastlog files have group write and global read permissions to allow for the lastlog function to perform. Limiting the permissions beyond this configuration will result in the failure of functions that rely on the lastlog database."
      solution    : "Configure the operating system to set permissions of all log files under the \"/var/log\" directory to \"640\" or more restricted by using the following command:

Note: The btmp, wtmp, and lastlog files are excluded. Refer to the Discussion for details.

$ sudo find /var/log -perm /137 ! -name '*[bw]tmp' ! -name '*lastlog' -type f -exec chmod 640 '{}' \\;"
      reference   : "800-53|SI-11a.,800-53|SI-11b.,800-53r5|SI-11a.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001312,CSCv7|14.6,CSCv8|3.3,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SI-11b.,ITSG-33|SI-11c.,LEVEL|2A,Rule-ID|SV-270756r1066757_rule,STIG-ID|UBTU-24-700010,Vuln-ID|V-270756"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22775"
      cmd         : "/bin/find /var/log -perm /137 ! -name '*[bw]tmp' ! -name '*lastlog' -type f -exec stat -c \"%n %a\" {} \\;  | /bin/awk '{print} END {if(NR==0) print \"none found\" }'"
      expect      : "^none found$"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : FIND_CMD
          description : "files"
          not_expect  : ".+"
          find_type   : "f"
          perm        : "/137"
          target      : "/run/log/journal" || "/var/log/journal"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FIND_CMD
          description : "directories"
          not_expect  : ".+"
          find_type   : "d"
          perm        : "/5137"
          target      : "/run/log/journal" || "/var/log/journal"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.114 UBTU-24-700020"
          info        : "The operating system must generate system journal entries without revealing information that could be exploited by adversaries.

GROUP ID: V-270757
RULE ID: SV-270757r1066760

Any operating system providing too much information in error messages risks compromising the data and security of the structure, and content of error messages needs to be carefully considered by the organization.

Organizations carefully consider the structure/content of error messages. The extent to which information systems are able to identify and handle error conditions is guided by organizational policy and operational requirements. Information that could be exploited by adversaries includes, for example, erroneous logon attempts with passwords entered by mistake as the username, mission/business information that can be derived from (if not stated explicitly by) information recorded, and personal information, such as account numbers, social security numbers, and credit card numbers."
          solution    : "Configure the operating system to set the appropriate permissions to the files and directories used by the systemd journal:

Add or modify the following lines in the \"/usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/systemd.conf\" file:

z /run/log/journal 2750 root systemd-journal - -
Z /run/log/journal/%m ~2750 root systemd-journal - -
z /var/log/journal 2750 root systemd-journal - -
z /var/log/journal/%m 2750 root systemd-journal - -
z /var/log/journal/%m/system.journal 0750 root systemd-journal - -

Restart the system for the changes to take effect."
          reference   : "800-53|SI-11a.,800-53|SI-11b.,800-53r5|SI-11a.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001312,CSCv7|14.6,CSCv8|3.3,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SI-11b.,ITSG-33|SI-11c.,LEVEL|2A,Rule-ID|SV-270757r1066760_rule,STIG-ID|UBTU-24-700020,Vuln-ID|V-270757"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22775"
          show_output : YES
        </report>
      </then>
    </if>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CHECK
      description : "1.115 UBTU-24-700030"
      info        : "The operating system must be configured so that the \"journalctl\" command is not accessible by unauthorized users.

GROUP ID: V-270758
RULE ID: SV-270758r1066763

Any operating system providing too much information in error messages risks compromising the data and security of the structure, and content of error messages needs to be carefully considered by the organization.

Organizations carefully consider the structure/content of error messages. The extent to which information systems are able to identify and handle error conditions is guided by organizational policy and operational requirements. Information that could be exploited by adversaries includes, for example, erroneous logon attempts with passwords entered by mistake as the username, mission/business information that can be derived from (if not stated explicitly by) information recorded, and personal information, such as account numbers, social security numbers, and credit card numbers."
      solution    : "Configure \"journalctl\" to have a permission set of \"740\":

$ sudo chmod 740 /usr/bin/journalctl"
      reference   : "800-53|SI-11a.,800-53|SI-11b.,800-53r5|SI-11a.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001312,CSCv7|14.6,CSCv8|3.3,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SI-11b.,ITSG-33|SI-11c.,LEVEL|2A,Rule-ID|SV-270758r1066763_rule,STIG-ID|UBTU-24-700030,Vuln-ID|V-270758"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22775"
      file        : "/usr/bin/journalctl"
      mask        : "037"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CHECK
      description : "1.116 UBTU-24-700040"
      info        : "The operating system must be configured so that the \"journalctl\" command is owned by \"root\".

GROUP ID: V-270759
RULE ID: SV-270759r1068367

Only authorized personnel should be aware of errors and the details of the errors. Error messages are an indicator of an organization's operational state or can identify the operating system or platform. Additionally, Personally identifiable information (PII) and operational information must not be revealed through error messages to unauthorized personnel or their designated representatives.

The structure and content of error messages must be carefully considered by the organization and development team. The extent to which the information system is able to identify and handle error conditions is guided by organizational policy and operational requirements."
      solution    : "Configure \"journalctl\" to be owned by \"root\":

$ sudo chown root /usr/bin/journalctl"
      reference   : "800-53|SI-11b.,800-53r5|SI-11b.,800-53r5|SI-11c.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001314,CSCv7|14.6,CSCv8|3.3,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SI-11c.,LEVEL|2A,Rule-ID|SV-270759r1068367_rule,STIG-ID|UBTU-24-700040,Vuln-ID|V-270759"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22775"
